task: json-strict-staged (BACKLOG json-fast-read's named next lane)
agent: okay-42
started: 2026-09-07
branch: feature/json-strict-staged

why: Json.readStrict reads 743.9 ns / 5048 B against circe's 813.1 /
  3416: the time is won, the bytes are 1.48x. The strict Schema walk
  costs about 3.3x the bare value parse (227 ns / 2208 B), and what
  it spends is the walk's own machinery -- a Map of found fields, a
  Vector of erased parts, p.make through Mirror -- none of which a
  reader that knows the type at compile time needs. The Staged macro
  already generates exactly that reader for a Json value
  (Staged.read(j)) and for CBOR bytes (Staged.read(in)); this lane
  gives it a third target, JsonStrict's Reader.

what: a `StrictJsonCodec[A]` from `Staged.derive[A]` (name settled in
  the lane) whose `decode(text): Either[String, A]` is generated code
  over JsonStrict.Reader's primitives -- products read field by field
  into locals and constructed directly, sums by the one-entry-object
  name, options/lists/vectors/iso/bytes/chars as the interpreted
  reader does -- so nothing is boxed into Any and nothing is looked up
  by name at run time that the type already knew.

the law: staged.decode(text) == Json.readStrict(text) on
  TestJsonStrict's corpus, well-formed and damaged alike -- the staged
  reader is the interpreted one's answer, faster. Measured on
  CodecBenchmark against textToOrderStrict, textToOrderCirce and the
  existing textToOrderStaged (parseValue + staged decode of the tree).

not touched: Json.readStrict stays as the interpreted door; the staged
  one is for a caller who derives a codec once and decodes many times.
